<div class="page">
  <div style="padding-top: 20px;">
    <h2 class="section-heading" style="font-size: 20pt;">EARNING UNIVERSITY CREDITS</h2>
    <div class="body-text">
      <h3 class="sub-heading">WHAT IS A CREDIT?</h3>
      <p>At students can earn university <span data-field="credits">{{ credits }}</span> that count toward their bachelor's degree. This means you can begin meeting the requirements of your undergraduate degree program while you are enrolled at the ELI.</p>
      <h3 class="sub-heading">WHY IS EARNING UNIVERSITY CREDITS HELPFUL?</h3>
      <p>Most undergraduate courses are worth 3 credits (though some are worth 4 credits). University students need to earn a certain number of credits (and complete a specific set of courses) in order to earn their bachelor's degree. As a result, the number of credits earned is a helpful way to track a student's progress towards graduation.</p>
      <p style="font-size: 10pt; margin-top: 20px;">*Business majors will take AT2 Cohort for business for not credit at the request of the Alfred Lerner College of Business and Economics.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>




<div class="page">
  <p>Credits also help count how many classes students have completed. Universities typically use the number of credits that students earn as a way to determine their status as freshmen, sophomores, juniors or seniors. For example, here is how student credits and class level are determined at the University of <span data-field="university_name">{{ university_name }}</span>:</p>

