  <h2>TAKE ADVANTAGE OF "MAJOR-SPECIFIC" ADVISING</h2>
  <p>As an AT student, you can receive advising that is specifically focused on the requirements of your major!</p>
  <p>The purpose of major-specific academic advising is to help students to start planning for their major at UD. AT major advisors work closely with UD academic advisors to help make the transition from AT to UD as smooth as possible. They can help you with any questions that you have about the requirements of your major, the UD courses you will need to take, and how to make the connection with your major's department at UD.</p>
  <p>Contact <span data-field="advising_contact">{{ advising_contact }}</span> at <span data-field="advising_email">{{ advising_email }}</span> or visit the Academic Success Center (ASC) to connect with your major advisor. Majors are grouped by college: Agriculture and Natural Resources, Arts and Sciences, Business, and Engineering.</p>
</div>

<div class="page">
  <h2>AT MINIMUM 2.0 GPA REQUIREMENT</h2>
  <p>In addition to the specific credit requirements previously explained, students must also achieve a GPA of at least 2.0 in all classes taken in AT1, AT2 and AT3 (if taken) to graduate from the ELI and start their studies at UD. This is your cumulative AT GPA and it refers to your grades in all classes taken while you are in AT1, AT2 and AT3 (if you take AT3).</p>
  <p>This should not be difficult for you—remember that you need a minimum grade of C+ to earn credit for a class. The grade C+ is a 2.3 GPA. The GPA of a 2.0 is a C average. To understand how to convert letter grades (like A-, B-, etc.) into GPA, this GPA calculator can help: <span data-field="gpa_calculator_url">{{ gpa_calculator_url }}</span>.</p>

  <h2>WHY DO I NEED A 2.0 MINIMUM GPA?</h2>
  <p>Sometimes students will not try hard in one class so that they can do well in pass another. However, this is not a successful college strategy because it can hurt your GPA. For example, if you earn a C+ in ARSC 150 and a D- in ARSC 151 (one of your LS classes), your GPA will fall to a 1.5. It will be very difficult to improve that GPA to the necessary 2.0 minimum.</p>
  <p>At UD, a cumulative GPA of 2.0 or better is required both to remain in good academic standing and is required for graduation. At UD, a GPA below 2.0 places a student on academic probation and they must raise their GPA to graduate. At UD, if a student's GPA falls below a 2.0, failing grades also earn UD students negative GPA points. If a UD student has too many of these, they face dismissal. Since the AT program prepares students for future success at UD, the same 2.0 minimum GPA graduation requirement applies in AT.</p>

  <h2>HOW DO I KNOW WHAT MY AT GPA IS?</h2>
  <p>If you have studied at the ELI as a Pre-AT student (when you took language courses in level 5, for example, not for UD credit), you will have an ELI GPA from your classes taken before you started AT. Pre-AT classes do not count in your AT GPA, but all AT1, AT2 and AT3 (if you take AT3) classes do. Again, your AT GPA refers to your grades in all classes taken in AT. You can use the GPA Calculator here to calculate your AT GPA: <span data-field="at_gpa_calculator_url">{{ at_gpa_calculator_url }}</span>.</p>

  <h2>WHAT IF MY GPA IS LOWER THAN 2.0?</h2>
  <p>If you have earned the minimum number of credits you need but your cumulative AT GPA is below a 2.0, then you will need to continue in AT and take classes to improve your GPA until you meet your GPA requirement. You cannot graduate from AT and matriculate to UD unless you have an AT GPA of 2.0 or higher.</p>
</div>

<div class="page">
  <h2>FIRST SEMESTER: AT1</h2>
  <h3>WHAT IS AT1?</h3>
  <p>Welcome to your first semester of the Academic Transitions program! AT1 courses are designed to help you to build a foundation in English for academic purposes that you will need to succeed at the university level. You can earn UD credits for the courses you take in all semesters of the AT program. Your grades in these classes will be part of your grade point average (GPA) while you are an ELI student, and will continue to be part of your GPA when you matriculate to UD. For more helpful information about GPA's, see Appendix I at the end of this handbook.</p>

  <h2>AT1 COURSES</h2>
  <p>AT1 is comprised of two main courses: ARSC 150 and ARSC 151.</p>

  <h3>ARSC 150: FOUNDATIONS OF ENGLISH FOR ACADEMIC PURPOSES (3 CREDITS)</h3>
  <p>ARSC 150 is an integrated-skills class (i.e., it is comprised of reading, writing, listening, grammar, vocabulary and speaking) that uses academic content to develop all areas of language proficiency while also building the background knowledge and study skills for success as an undergraduate student in the U.S. The goal of this course is to teach students to understand, think about, summarize and respond critically to challenging written and spoken texts on topics of academic and public interest.</p>

  <h3>ARSC 151: ELI CULTURAL ELECTIVES (3 CREDITS)</h3>
  <p>In order to receive credit for ARSC 151, a student must receive a grade of C+ or better in two single session Listening/Speaking courses offered at the AT1 level. Furthermore, students cannot enroll in the same course for both sessions of ARSC151. For example, a student may not enroll in drama for both sessions of ARSC151.</p>
</div>

<div class="page">
  <h2>THE COHORT EXPERIENCE</h2>
  <p>All AT students participate in the ELI's renowned Cohort experience. This program helps students to develop the important personal, social, cultural and academic skills that are necessary for success at the university level. New AT students begin Cohort during AT1. More information about Cohort is available later in this handbook.</p>

  <h2>UNIVERSITY CREDITS EARNED IN AT1</h2>
  <p><strong>IMPORTANT NOTE</strong></p>
  <p>Students must earn a minimum grade of C+ in order to receive credit for any AT or UD content course.</p>
  <p>A unique feature of the AT program is the option of Grade Replacement. If a student fails a class by earning a grade below a C-, the student may retake the class one time. The new and better grade (C+ or better) then replaces the failing grade. Please note that as decided by UD, the Grade Replacement Policy does not currently extend to grades of C or C-.</p>

  <h2>ADVANCING TO AT2: THINGS TO KNOW</h2>
  <p>Students must pass ARSC 150 with a minimum score of C+ in order to advance to AT2.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Any student enrolled in ARSC 150 is automatically considered an AT1 student. Students will not be considered to be in AT2 until they are enrolled in the ELI ENGL 110 course (see AT2 section in this handbook).</li>
    <li>Students who pass ARSC 150 (minimum score: C+) but who do not pass ARSC 151 (minimum score: C+) and/or Cohort (minimum score: C+) will not be eligible to advance to AT2.</li>
    <li>These students will still need to meet all matriculation requirements (including passing ARSC 151 and Cohort) in order to complete the AT program.</li>
    <li>More information about matriculation requirements can be found in the AT2 and AT3 sections in this handbook.</li>
    <li>Students have only two chances to pass an AT course with a C- or otherwise, they may be dismissed from the ELI and lose their conditional admission to UD.</li>
    <li>Remember that to graduate, you need a cumulative AT GPA of 2.0 or better. Start working on your GPA now!</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>WHAT IF I DO NOT EARN THE REQUIRED GRADES TO ADVANCE TO AT2?</h3>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Course</th>
        <th>You must...</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>ARSC 150</td>
        <td>Repeat ARSC 150 in another AT1 semester. This will add another semester to your AT program and will postpone when you start full-time study at UD.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>ARSC 151</td>
        <td>Repeat ARSC 151.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>(the AT1/LS requirement)</td>
        <td>Failing ARSC 151 means failing to earn the minimum required C+ grade in 1 or both of the 2 LS classes that comprise this requirement. Passing 1 LS/S class but failing the other means this requirement has not been met.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td></td>
        <td>If you do not pass ARSC 151 in AT1, then you cannot take content courses (other than number of credits to graduate).</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td></td>
        <td>If you failed ARSC 150 and ARSC 151 (AT1 Cohort requirement), then you will repeat both ARSC 150 and ARSC 151 in AT1 again.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Cohort</td>
        <td>Repeat AT1 Cohort.</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
</div>

<div class="page">
  <h2>SECOND SEMESTER: AT2</h2>
  <h3>WHAT IS AT2?</h3>
  <p>In this semester, you will take ENGL 110: Seminar in Composition (which is required of all UD students) as well as other credit-bearing university content courses.</p>

  <h2>AT2 COURSES & ACTIVITIES</h2>
  <p>In AT2, you will take three classes (all carry UD credit) and AT2 Cohort for Business or UNIV101:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Seminar in Composition for Academic Transitions (also known as ELI ENGL 110)</li>
    <li>Two credit-bearing content courses that can meet requirements for your degree and/or for your major</li>
    <li>Each UD class will have a lecture and a discussion class.</li>
    <li>AT2 Cohort for Business or UNIV101</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>UNIVERSITY CONTENT COURSES</h3>
  <p>What is a university content course?</p>
  <p>A university content course is a class in which you study an academic topic like math, political science or business administration. As an AT student, you will begin taking more of the university courses that you will need to complete as part of the requirements for your bachelor's degree and/or for your major. All course objectives and expectations are identical to the equivalent course that is being offered to U.S. students and directly admitted international students at UD.</p>
  <p>AT2 students take two university content courses (earning up to 6 credits). The specific courses that they take will depend on their intended major. To see a list of the available courses, please turn to Appendix II later in this handbook. For additional details about these courses (including prerequisites and restrictions), see the ELI Course Catalogue at <span data-field="eli_course_catalogue_url">{{ eli_course_catalogue_url }}</span>.</p>
  <p>To provide you with extra English-language support, you will have additional class meetings called discussion classes. These meetings will help you develop the study skills and knowledge needed to meet course objectives. This is a great advantage of taking these required classes while you are an ELI student!</p>

  <h3>Who teaches content courses?</h3>
  <p>All courses are taught by subject-matter experts (i.e., a person with strong knowledge of the specific topic being taught) who are approved by the relevant University department. For example, this means that students who enroll in an economics course are taught by an instructor with expertise in economics.</p>

  <h3>ELI ENGL 110: SEMINAR IN COMPOSITION FOR AT (3 CREDITS)</h3>
  <p>The ENGL 110 Seminar in Composition is a required course that all first year UD students must take. The purpose of this class is to introduce students to key genres of university-level academic writing, including the personal essay, summary, critical interpretation, argument and research paper. Students read, understand, synthesize and respond critically to challenging texts on topics of public, professional, disciplinary, and personal interest.</p>
</div>

<div class="page">
  <h2>DISCUSSION CLASSES (REQUIRED)</h2>
  <p>All AT students have 2 hours of discussion classes per week for each university course that they are taking. In these classes, which are taught by Instructional Assistants (IAs), students review the content from the course lectures, receive feedback on upcoming assignments, and learn how to manage the workload for the class. This helps students to improve their note-taking and listening comprehension skills, academic reading skills, vocabulary needed for the course, and study skills. By actively participating in discussion classes, students are better able to earn a higher grade than they would on their own because they have help managing their time, understanding the course content, and meeting the professor's expectations.</p>
  <p>Discussion classes are co-requisites of the university courses. This means that you are required to take the discussion class with each UD content course that you are taking.</p>

  <h2>THE COHORT EXPERIENCE</h2>
